        <!-- SECTION 1: Chemistry Types -->
        <div id="chemistry-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Epoxy vs Polyaspartic vs Polyurea vs Polyurethane: What Actually Separates Them
          </h2>
          <p>
            Every concrete floor coating on the market is built from one of four base chemistries, and understanding the real differences between them — not the marketing language stamped on the can — is the single most useful thing you can learn before spending money on a floor. This applies whether you're coating a residential garage, a damp basement slab, a home workshop, or a commercial or industrial floor that needs to handle forklift traffic and chemical spills.
          </p>

          <!-- Epoxy -->
          <div class="mt-10">
            <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">Epoxy: Affordable, Moderate Cure, Moderate UV Stability</h3>
            <p>
              Epoxy is a two-part thermoset system — an epoxide resin (Part A) reacts with an amine hardener (Part B) to form a hard, cross-linked polymer that mechanically bonds to properly prepared concrete. It remains the most widely used floor coating chemistry because it's genuinely affordable and forgiving to apply, with water-based versions offering a 2–4 hour pot life that even a first-time DIYer can manage.
            </p>
            <p>
              The trade-offs are real. Standard epoxy requires <strong>24–72 hours</strong> before the floor can handle foot traffic and vehicle parking, and its aromatic chemistry is inherently UV-sensitive — direct sunlight breaks down the amine-epoxide bonds over time, producing the yellowing and chalking that shows up on epoxy floors near garage doors and windows within a year or two. Epoxy is the right default chemistry for budget-conscious residential garages and basements with limited sun exposure, but it's rarely the first choice for commercial floors or any space with heavy sunlight exposure.
            </p>
          </div>

          <!-- Polyaspartic -->
          <div class="mt-10">
            <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">Polyaspartic: Fast Cure, Excellent UV Stability, Higher Cost</h3>
            <p>
              Polyaspartic coatings are an aliphatic sub-category of polyurea chemistry, built from an aspartic ester resin reacting with an aliphatic isocyanate. That aliphatic backbone is the key difference from epoxy: it doesn't absorb UV energy the way epoxy's aromatic rings do, which means polyaspartic coatings hold their color and gloss indefinitely under direct sunlight — no yellowing, no chalking, regardless of exposure.
            </p>
            <p>
              The other headline advantage is cure speed. Quality polyaspartic systems are drive-on ready in <strong>four to eight hours</strong>, compared to a full 72 hours for water-based epoxy — a genuinely important difference for a household with one garage bay and no alternative parking, or a commercial space that can't stay closed for days. The cost is the trade-off: expect to pay roughly <strong>two to three times</strong> as much per square foot as epoxy. For sun-exposed floors, showroom garages, and any project where downtime has a real cost, polyaspartic is usually worth the premium.
            </p>
          </div>

          <!-- Polyurea -->
          <div class="mt-10">
            <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">Polyurea: Extremely Fast Cure, Flexible, Commercial & Industrial Grade</h3>
            <p>
              Pure polyurea takes the polyaspartic idea further: it reacts an isocyanate component with an amine-terminated resin in a chemical reaction that's dramatically faster than either epoxy or polyaspartic — full cure can happen in under an hour, and the coating is often tack-free within minutes of application. That reaction speed is also why pure polyurea is typically installed by professional crews using heated, plural-component spray equipment rather than rolled on from a bucket the way DIY epoxy and polyaspartic kits are.
            </p>
            <p>
              The other defining property of polyurea is <strong>flexibility</strong>. Where epoxy is a rigid, brittle film that can crack when the underlying concrete shifts or a slab develops hairline cracks, polyurea has enough elongation to stretch and move with the substrate without fracturing. This makes it the standard choice for demanding commercial and industrial floors — parking garages, loading docks, manufacturing floors, and secondary containment areas — where thermal cycling, vibration, and heavy point loads would eventually crack a rigid epoxy coating. It's overkill (and expensive) for a typical residential garage, but it's the right chemistry when the floor sees industrial-grade abuse.
            </p>
          </div>

          <!-- Polyurethane -->
          <div class="mt-10">
            <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">Polyurethane: The Topcoat That Upgrades Any Base Coat</h3>
            <p>
              Polyurethane is usually not applied as a standalone floor coating — it's applied as a clear or pigmented <strong>topcoat</strong> over a cured epoxy, polyaspartic, or polyurea base coat to add a wear layer that the base coat alone doesn't provide. Aliphatic polyurethane topcoats are significantly harder-wearing against abrasion than epoxy on its own, resist scuffing from foot and cart traffic exceptionally well, and — like polyaspartic — hold their color under UV exposure without yellowing.
            </p>
            <p>
              The practical case for adding a polyurethane topcoat is straightforward: it extends the service life of a cheaper base coat, protects decorative color flakes or metallic pigments from wear, and gives you a sacrificial surface layer that takes the abuse instead of the structural coating underneath. Most professional installations — and any serious commercial floor — use a polyurethane or polyaspartic topcoat over an epoxy or polyurea base rather than leaving the base coat exposed as the final wear surface.
            </p>
          </div>

