          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Which Coating Chemistry Fits Your Floor?
          </h2>
          <p>
            The right chemistry depends far more on where the floor is and what happens on it than on any single brand's marketing claims. Use these scenarios to match your actual space to the chemistry built to handle it.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 mt-8">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-black text-lg text-gray-900 mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">
                <span class="text-[#FF4500] font-black">01</span> Residential Garage, Budget-Conscious
              </h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">A typical one- or two-car garage with limited sun exposure and a budget that matters. <strong>Recommended: Water-based epoxy (Rust-Oleum EpoxyShield).</strong> It's the least expensive true two-part chemistry, easy to apply, and performs well as long as UV exposure and cure-time patience aren't major factors.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-black text-lg text-gray-900 mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">
                <span class="text-[#FF4500] font-black">02</span> Basement Floor or Below-Grade Slab
              </h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Basements bring humidity and occasional moisture vapor concerns rather than UV exposure. <strong>Recommended: Water-based epoxy or polycuramine hybrid.</strong> UV stability matters less here than in a sun-exposed garage, so standard epoxy chemistry is usually adequate — just confirm the slab passes a moisture test before coating with any chemistry, since none of these products bond well over active moisture vapor transmission.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-black text-lg text-gray-900 mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">
                <span class="text-[#FF4500] font-black">03</span> Sun-Exposed Garage or Showroom Space
              </h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Large windows, frequently open doors, or a floor that's meant to look pristine for years. <strong>Recommended: Polyaspartic (Genesis 1-Day) or a polycuramine hybrid.</strong> Both chemistries resist the UV yellowing that will visibly age a straight epoxy floor within a year or two of consistent sun exposure.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-black text-lg text-gray-900 mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">
                <span class="text-[#FF4500] font-black">04</span> Home Workshop or Heavy-Use Garage
              </h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Dropped tools, chemical spills, rolling tool chests, and frequent heavy use. <strong>Recommended: High-solids epoxy (Epoxy-Coat Professional) topped with a polyurethane topcoat (Armorseal).</strong> The thicker epoxy base plus a hard, UV-stable wear layer delivers most of the durability of premium chemistries at a fraction of the cost of pure polyurea.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-black text-lg text-gray-900 mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">
                <span class="text-[#FF4500] font-black">05</span> Commercial, Warehouse, or Industrial Floor
              </h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Forklift traffic, chemical exposure, slab movement, or a facility that can't stay closed for days. <strong>Recommended: Pure polyurea (Rhino Linings), professionally installed.</strong> The flexibility and near-instant cure are specifically built for the loads and downtime constraints of commercial and industrial environments — epoxy and even polyaspartic are typically not durable or flexible enough for this tier of use.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 border-b pb-4">
            Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">What's the real difference between epoxy and polyaspartic?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">The chemistry is fundamentally different: epoxy is an aromatic epoxide-amine reaction, while polyaspartic is an aliphatic isocyanate reaction. In practice, that means polyaspartic cures dramatically faster (4–8 hours versus 24–72 hours for epoxy) and is fully UV-stable, while epoxy is more affordable and more forgiving to apply but will yellow under sustained UV exposure and takes far longer before you can park a vehicle or walk on it.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Is polyurea better than epoxy for a garage floor?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">For a typical residential garage, not necessarily — pure polyurea requires professional spray equipment and trained installers, and the cost per square foot is significantly higher than epoxy for performance most residential garages don't need. Polyurea earns its premium in commercial and industrial settings with heavy loads, slab movement, or extremely tight downtime windows. A hybrid polycuramine product or a topcoated epoxy system typically delivers better value for a home garage.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does polyaspartic take to cure compared to epoxy?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Quality polyaspartic systems are typically drive-on ready in <strong>4–8 hours</strong>, while standard water-based epoxy needs <strong>24–72 hours</strong> before it can handle vehicle traffic. Pure polyurea cures even faster — often tack-free within minutes — but it's applied by professional crews rather than DIYers. Full chemical cure for any of these chemistries generally takes several days longer than the drive-on timeframe.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you put a polyurethane topcoat over epoxy?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Yes, and it's a common professional practice. A polyurethane topcoat adds abrasion resistance and UV stability that epoxy alone doesn't have, extending the service life of the coating system and protecting any decorative color flakes broadcast into the base coat. For best adhesion, apply the topcoat while the epoxy base is still in its partially cured "green" state, or scuff-sand the base coat first if it has already fully cured.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Which coating chemistry is used in commercial and industrial buildings?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Commercial and industrial floors most commonly use pure polyurea, high-solids epoxy, or polyaspartic base coats, frequently finished with a polyurethane or polyaspartic topcoat for the wear layer. Polyurea in particular is standard for parking structures, loading docks, and manufacturing floors because its flexibility and rapid cure handle heavy loads, slab movement, and tight downtime windows better than rigid epoxy alone.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Do polyaspartic and polyurea coatings need professional application?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Polyaspartic products marketed for DIY use, like consumer garage floor kits, can be rolled on by a confident homeowner — the working time per batch is shorter than epoxy, so prior coating experience helps. Pure polyurea, by contrast, is almost always installed by trained crews using heated, plural-component spray equipment because its reaction speed makes bucket-and-roller application impractical. If a product is labeled simply "polyurea" rather than "polyaspartic," assume it requires professional installation.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

